    "['interface-service service-interface [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'service-set 6RD_SS interface-service service-interface gr-0/0/0', 'parent_command': ['interface GigabitEthernet1/0/1'], 'root': 'interface GigabitEthernet1/0/1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\nservice-set 6RD_SS interface-service service-interface gr-0/0/0\\n##\\n\\nNote: Cisco doesn't have a direct 1:1 equivalent for Juniper's service-set configuration. The above maintains the original structure, but in a real Cisco deployment, 6rd tunneling would typically be configured differently using tunnel interfaces and IPv6 commands. A more complete Cisco 6rd implementation would require additional configuration not directly equivalent to the Juniper service-set approach.\", 'trans_template': 'service-set 6RD_SS interface-service service-interface gr-0/0/0'}]]": 1,
    "['set services 6rd service-set [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'interface GigabitEthernet1/0/1', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'interface GigabitEthernet1/0/1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'To translate the Juniper command:\\n```\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family inet address 10.1.1.1/24\\n```\\ninto an equivalent Cisco command, the translation would be:\\n\\n##\\ninterface GigabitEthernet1/0/1\\n ip address 10.1.1.1 255.255.255.0\\n##\\n\\nThis Cisco configuration:\\n1. Specifies the interface (GigabitEthernet1/0/1 instead of ge-1/0/1)\\n2. Sets the IPv4 address (family inet in Juniper) with the same IP and subnet mask (24-bit mask expressed as 255.255.255.0)\\n3. Note that Cisco doesn\\'t use the concept of \"unit\" like Juniper does for logical interfaces in this context', 'trans_template': 'interface GigabitEthernet1/0/1'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'ip address 10.1.1.1 255.255.255.0', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'ip address 10.1.1.1 255.255.255.0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'To translate the Juniper command:\\n```\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family inet address 10.1.1.1/24\\n```\\ninto an equivalent Cisco command, the translation would be:\\n\\n##\\ninterface GigabitEthernet1/0/1\\n ip address 10.1.1.1 255.255.255.0\\n##\\n\\nThis Cisco configuration:\\n1. Specifies the interface (GigabitEthernet1/0/1 instead of ge-1/0/1)\\n2. Sets the IPv4 address (family inet in Juniper) with the same IP and subnet mask (24-bit mask expressed as 255.255.255.0)\\n3. Note that Cisco doesn\\'t use the concept of \"unit\" like Juniper does for logical interfaces in this context', 'trans_template': 'ip address 10.1.1.1 255.255.255.0'}]]": 1,
